this is a production of cornell  university  great well good afternoon everyone i'm  excited to  talk to you about my dissertation  research today uh titled  genomics-assisted breeding of malting  barley for new york  state so by the year 2050 it's expected  that there will be 9 billion people  on this world and food production will  be challenged by  increased population climate change and  land degradation  that means well we're going to need more  beer  and that means we need more barley  barley is a diploid grass that's been  the primary ingredient in brewing and  distilling for millennia  there are two primary classifications of  barley the first is by how the grains  are arranged on the spike  and that's two row versus six row the  picture on the right shows two row  barley  and the second is by vernalization  requirement winter barley is planted in  the fall  and spring barley is planted in the  spring i will primarily be talking about  two-row spring malting barley today  in north america most barley production  is in the west in the united states  in montana idaho and north dakota and in  canada  and the prairie provinces of alberta  saskatchewan and manitoba  however new york state has a strong  barley heritage  in the mid-1800s new york was a barley  production powerhouse  with most with most acreage focused in  the western part of the state  along the erie canal this fed large malt  houses and breweries in buffalo  but continued westward agricultural  expansion  as well as a series of tariffs with  canada killed malting barley production  in the late 1800s  and barley has since languished as a  major crop in new york since then  but that's beginning to change in 2012  the new york state legislature passed  the farm brewery bill  this legislation created a new class of  craft brewery new york farm breweries  receive economic incentives and the fun  tagline of being a farm brewery  if they use locally grown grain and hops  in their  beers now the  in 2012 