ed in the  killing stage where dry heat is applied  and that also helps preserve enzyme  activity and develop color and flavor  so why do we care about enzyme activity  amylases are the enzymes that break down  starch into simpler sugars  and that actual conversion of starch to  sugar does not happen until the first  step of the brewing process  but malting kind of primes the grain in  order to  be able to make alcohol efficiently  there's a protective matrix of protein  and beta-glucan that surrounds the small  starch molecules within the barley grain  and during the germination process this  matrix is broken down  and amylase enzymes are activated this  is called modification and uniform  rapid germination of barley seeds in the  malt house  is needed for efficient complete  modification  malting quality is actually a suite of  10 to 12  quantitatively inherited traits that are  all related to  grain modification and grain chemistry  protein and starch  and as you might expect phenotyping is  slow  expensive and requires a lot of grain  much like other quality traits  seed dormancy is the inability of the  seed to germinate under otherwise  favorable conditions  and seed dormancy is a very important  consideration in malting  because malt houses require germination  to make malt  they require non-dormant grain and in  the context of malting there are  two primary phenotypes that we're  interested in when it comes to seed  dormancy  the first is germination energy this is  simply a measure of seed dormancy  that's measured as the germination  percent over three days  with a higher germination energy  indicating no dormancy  and germination index is a measure of  germination rate  over three days the higher germination  index  implying a higher germination rate  so these two traits are important for  malting and  but in new york for our germplasm and  for malt houses in general  we have a limited understanding of how  germination rate changes over time  we're intere