un because  there's a lot of cool stuff  in new york and it's worth exploring  and i would be happy to hear any  questions and comments  so thanks so much for your time thank  you very much dan  we're running a little bit long but if  uh people could take a few more minutes  to  um uh ask some questions  um dan would be glad to see if he can  answer those for you  okay so i see one question from the  mirror lab you generate a lot of data  how do you deal with the data management  challenge  that's a great question and i'll admit  that we did not do a great job of  managing data beforehand  i think that's something as a lab we  personally to work on but  my colleague travis journey pushed me  into using github so we've been using  github to manage and share our data and  code a little bit better so  we're working on that but that's  definitely still an active area  i will be uploading a lot of this data  to the t3 breed base  but that hasn't happened yet  well that's probably something i could  do better as an advisor as well  it's a big job any other questions from  anybody  yeah i have a question yeah go ahead  yeah uh so have you looked in the  candidate genes they involved in  aba or ga biosynthesis  and or signaling and looking to their  possible impact  on c dominancy in body  yeah that's a good question so there has  been a lot of work done in barley  looking  at things like aba hydroxylase and  the nced genes which are also involved  in aba metabolism  i did not look at expression of endless  genes specifically  but i think to get a really good but i  have a feeling that some of those genes  might be underlying some of that  background genetic variation for  temperature sensitivity  and because those genes are helping to  determine how much obscenic acid is in  the grain at any given time  so i didn't look into that specifically  but i think that's definitely an  opportunity for future research  okay thank you  we have another question from  travis travis you want to  ask your question verbally  i i can um so you mentioned the  multi-quality interactions that  um with mkkk3 alleles what have you  found and what are the implications for  the different types of uh brewing  yeah so i very briefly mentioned this  but our  early mulching quality mapping results  in the s2met population suggests that  uh the highly non-german n-star allele  is associated with  some grain modification traits like  soluble over total protein  work protein  and then freemium nitrogen  those are really important traits more  so  for industrial brewing by industrial  brewing i mean like anhyzer bush  miller coors so budweiser miller lite uh  anything like things like yingling um  whereas the craft mulching profile is a  little bit different  and we're thinking that uh  prayer of spreading resistance with the  other two mkk3 alleles  might not be compromising walton quality  and we also found multi-quality  qtl at non-sd2 loci  which is very promising for selecting  for pre-harvest grounding resistance and  good malting  okay well um i don't see any other  questions in the chat box  um i don't see anybody's hand up so  let's uh thank dan for an outstanding  exit seminar  and he'll be around for i think what a  couple more weeks right down  yep uh definitely through may and then  here and there in june  okay thanks very much and thank you  for attending dan's seminar  all right thanks everybody bye-bye  this has been a production of cornell  university  on the web at cornell.edu


